In our setting, quasi-concavity is evaluated with respect to an individual agent’s mixed strategy. To ensure this is welldefined, we fix the strategies of all other agents. This is a standard approach in both game-theoretic best-response analysis and parametric model checking [Shoham and Leyton-Brown, 2009; Basset et al., 2015]. When both reward and responsibility allocation are linear in the agent’s strategy, the resulting utility function is quasi-concave, enabling NE computation via convex optimisation. In a PSMAS, memoryless strategies are encoded symbolically by parameters denoting action probabilities. With the other agents’ strategies fixed, quasi-concavity follows whenever the reward and responsibility terms are linear in the strategy parameters of the agent under consideration. We show that, for reachability and safety objectives in our setting, these terms satisfy the required conditions, yielding the following result.
